:" list the G-moves first, ","type":"text"},{"code":"(g′, h)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" for each entry g′ of G’s row of g, then the H-moves","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"code":"(g, h′)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":". They are computed from the factors’ index rows and never hash. A factor’s self-loop","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"gives a product self-loop, listed twice in an undirected row as the ","type":"text"},{"code":"Graph","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" law asks.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"strong","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Edges"}]},{"text":" are named by the factor edge and the factor vertex that generate them:","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".first(edge: e, vertexIndex: h)"},{"text":" is G’s edge ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"e"},{"text":" at H’s vertex index ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"h"},{"text":", and","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".second(vertexIndex: g, edge: e)"},{"text":" is H’s edge ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"e"},{"text":" at G’s vertex index ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"g"},{"text":". ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"edges"},{"text":" lists every","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".first"},{"text":" edge, G-edge outer, then every ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".second"},{"text":" edge, G-vertex outer. When both factors have","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"edge indices, so does the product: ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"index(e)·|V(H)| + h"},{"text":", then ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"m_G·|V(H)| + g·m_H + index(e)"},{"text":".","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"Products of multigraphs and pseudographs are defined edge by edge, so ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"edgeCount"},{"text":", the rows and","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"the degrees always agree.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"To materialize it, pass it to a representation’s initializer. Products are "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Sendable"},{"type":"text","text":" when both"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"factors are."}]}]}],"variants":[{"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}],"paths":["\/documentation\/graphproducts\/cartesianproductview"]}],"topicSections":[{"identifiers":["doc:\/\/GraphProducts\/documentation\/GraphProducts\/CartesianProductView\/DirectedEdges","doc:\/\/GraphProducts\/documentation\/GraphProducts\/CartesianProductView\/EdgeIndexRow","doc:\/\/GraphProducts\/documentation\/GraphProducts\/CartesianProductView\/EdgeRow","doc:\/\/GraphProducts\/documentation\/GraphProducts\/CartesianProductView\/IndexRow","doc:\/\/GraphProducts\/documentation\/GraphProducts\/CartesianProductView\/UndirectedEdges"],"title":"Structures","generated":true,"anchor":"Structures"},{"identifiers":["doc:\/\/GraphProducts\/documentation\/GraphProducts\/CartesianProductView\/first","doc:\/\/GraphProducts\/documentation\/GraphProducts\/CartesianProductView\/second"],"title":"Instance Properties","generated":true,"anchor":"Instance-Properties"},{"identifiers":["doc:\/\/GraphProducts\/documentation\/GraphProducts\/CartesianProductView\/Position"],"title":"Enumerations","generated":true,"anchor":"Enumerations"},{"identifiers":["doc:\/\/GraphProducts\/documentation\/GraphProducts\/CartesianProductView\/BidirectionalDirectedGraph-Implementations","doc:\/\/GraphProducts\/documentation\/GraphProducts\/CartesianProductView\/DirectedGraph-Implementations","doc:\/\/GraphProducts\/documentation\/GraphProducts\/CartesianProductView\/Graph-Implementations"],"title":"Default Implementations","generated":true,"anchor":"Default-Implementations"}],"kind":"symbol","abstract":[{"text":"The Cartesian product G □ H of two graphs